## DESCRIPTION
## Calculus Two, Sequences
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Infinite sequences and series)
## DBsection(Limit of a sequence)
## Date(09/16/2018)
## Institution(Community College of Denver, Colorado Community College System)
## Author(Brittni Lorton)
## KEYWORDS('calculus', 'sequences', 'identify bounded', 'identify monotonicity')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "parserPopUp.pl", "PGcourse.pl",);

Context('Numeric')->variables->are(n => "Real");

$a      = random(2, 6);
$popup1 = DropDown([ "is", "is not" ], "is");
$popup2 =
    DropDown([ "monotone increasing", "monotone decreasing", "not monotone" ],
    "monotone decreasing");

BEGIN_PGML

For the sequence  [`\{a_n\} = \{n^{([$a]/n)}\}`], for [`n\geq 3`], determine whether the sequence is bounded and whether it is eventually monotone increasing, monotone decreasing, or not monotone. 

a) [`\{a_n\} = \{n^{([$a]/n)}\}`] [___]{$popup1} bounded.

b) [`\{a_n\} = \{n^{([$a]/n)}\}`] is eventually  [___]{$popup2} .

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
If we rewrite
[``` a_n = e^{ln(n^{[$a]/n})} = e^{\frac{5 \ln n}{n}}```]
then because 
[``` \frac{5 \ln n}{n}```] is eventually monotonically decreasing, the original sequence is as well. 

If the latter step isn't clear, then write [``f(x) = \frac{5 \ln x}{x}``] and find the derivative or 
[``` f'(x) = \frac{x\frac{5}{x} - 5 \ln x}{x^2} = \frac{5-5\ln x}{x^2} ```]
and this is negative for all [`x \geq e^5`].  If a function is negative, then its corresponding sequence is decreasing.  

Lastly, since [`n`] is positive, the sequence is always positive so bounded below by 0 and above by one of the first few terms. 
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
